{"name":"Supreme Pontiff","hex":"#d4d8db","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"supreme","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Dominant, having power over all others.","Greatest, or the most excellent, extreme, superior, highest, or utmost."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To divide a citrus fruit into its segments, removing the skin, pith, membranes, and seeds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/supreme"},{"term":"pontiff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the most illustrious of the colleges of priests of the Roman religion, the College of Pontiffs; a pontifex. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A bishop of the early Church; now specifically, the Pope."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pontiff"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pope","blocks":[{"text":"The pope is the bishop of Rome and the head of the worldwide Catholic Church. He is also known as the supreme pontiff, Roman pontiff, or sovereign pontiff. From the 8th century until 1870, the pope was the sovereign or head of state of the Papal States, and since 1929 of the much smaller Vatican City State. From a Catholic viewpoint, the primacy of the bishop of Rome is largely derived from his role as the apostolic successor to Saint Peter, to whom primacy was conferred by Jesus, who gave Peter the Keys of Heaven and the powers of \"binding and loosing\", naming him as the \"rock\" upon which the Church would be built. The current pope is Leo XIV, who was elected on 8 May 2025 on the second day of the 2025 papal conclave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Pope_Leo_XIV_3_%283x4_cropped%29.png/330px-Pope_Leo_XIV_3_%283x4_cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope","wikidata":"Q19546"},{"title":"Pontiff","blocks":[{"text":"In Roman antiquity, a pontiff was a member of the most illustrious of the colleges of priests of the Roman religion, the College of Pontiffs. The term pontiff was later applied to any high or chief priest and, in Catholic ecclesiastical usage, to bishops, especially the Pope, who is sometimes referred to as the Roman Pontiff or the Supreme Pontiff."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Statue_of_Augustus_as_Pontifex_Maximus%2C_last_decade_of_1st_cent._BCE%3B_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme%2C_Rome_%282%29.jpg/330px-Statue_of_Augustus_as_Pontifex_Maximus%2C_last_decade_of_1st_cent._BCE%3B_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme%2C_Rome_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontiff","wikidata":"Q13365614"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Gesupietrochiave.jpg/500px-Gesupietrochiave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Gesupietrochiave.jpg/500px-Gesupietrochiave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGesupietrochiave.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Gregorythegreat.jpg/500px-Gregorythegreat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Gregorythegreat.jpg/500px-Gregorythegreat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gregory the Great (c. 540–604), in a painting by Carlo Saraceni, c. 1610, Rome","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGregorythegreat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Mediterranean1400.png/500px-Mediterranean1400.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Mediterranean1400.png/500px-Mediterranean1400.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map of the Mediterranean states in 1400. The East-West Schism began in 1054 and continues, while the Western Schism lasted from 1378 to 1417.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMediterranean1400.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Council_Trent.jpg/250px-Council_Trent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Council_Trent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Council_Trent.jpg/250px-Council_Trent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"As part of the Catholic Reformation, Pope Paul III (1534–1549) initiated the Council of Trent (1545–1563), which established the triumph of the papacy over those who sought to reconcile with Protestants or oppose papal claims.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACouncil_Trent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Michelangelo%2C_lunetta%2C_Zerubbabel_-_Abiud_-_Eliakim_01.jpg/500px-Michelangelo%2C_lunetta%2C_Zerubbabel_-_Abiud_-_Eliakim_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Michelangelo%2C_lunetta%2C_Zerubbabel_-_Abiud_-_Eliakim_01.jpg/500px-Michelangelo%2C_lunetta%2C_Zerubbabel_-_Abiud_-_Eliakim_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fresco of Eliakim in the Sistine Chapel, Vatican City","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMichelangelo%2C_lunetta%2C_Zerubbabel_-_Abiud_-_Eliakim_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Christ_Handing_the_Keys_to_St._Peter_by_Pietro_Perugino.jpg/500px-Christ_Handing_the_Keys_to_St._Peter_b